= Modal logic — Technical =

== Kripke frame ==
A frame is:

<pre>
F = (W,R)
</pre>

where W is a set of worlds/states and R is an accessibility relation.

A model adds a valuation V.

== Semantics ==
For model M:

<pre>
M,w ⊨ □φ
iff for every v with wRv, M,v ⊨ φ
</pre>

and:

<pre>
M,w ⊨ ◇φ
iff there exists v with wRv and M,v ⊨ φ
</pre>

== Frame conditions ==
Different axiom systems correspond to properties of R.

Examples:

* T — reflexivity;
* S4 — reflexivity + transitivity;
* S5 — equivalence-like accessibility in standard presentations.

Correspondence results connect syntax to frame structure.

== Modal variants ==
Epistemic, temporal, deontic, dynamic, provability, and description logics reuse modal techniques with different interpretations and axioms.

== Expressive/computational tradeoffs ==
Modal logics often balance greater expressive power against decidability and complexity.

Some fragments have efficient decision procedures; richer combinations can become difficult or undecidable.

